00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:28.480
This is the AI briefing deep dive for Friday, November 6, Claude and the Vault.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.460
What an AI did in the lab.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.140
I'm Bella.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.700
Like the briefing, it is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:43.060
On November 4, Anthropics Biology Research Lab published a note

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.980
claiming the first 3D structure of a vault particle from a prokaryote,

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.300
a simple single-celled organism like a bacterium.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:56.440
Vaults are huge, hollow barrels of protein found in cells from slime molds to humans.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:00.960
They were discovered in 1986, and nobody knows what they do.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.420
Anthropic says Claude worked at every step,

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.480
searching protein databases, designing DNA,

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.760
tuning the purification and processing the microscope data.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:14.880
It reports that purification fell from three days to about 90 minutes.

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:18.480
It says a first structural model was ready in 34 hours.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.520
Anthropic estimates that would normally have taken weeks.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.440
The same day, Nature published AutoDNA,

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.360
a lab from Shanghai Jiao Tong University,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.540
where AI coordinates more than 20 types of instruments.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:35.780
Two very different claims about AI doing lab work came out together.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.160
So it's a good moment to sort out which parts can be checked,

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.200
and which parts are a lab grading its own homework.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.240
Start with the object.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.220
A vault looks like a ribbed barrel with domed caps

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.800
built from many copies of one protein locked together.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:53.660
The human version uses 78 copies.

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:56.560
Anthropics version comes from a bacteriophage,

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.380
a virus that infects bacteria,

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.220
so the vault gets built inside a bacterial cell.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.140
Its protein, which the team calls L5,

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:07.500
assembles into 96 copies, 48 in each half.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.160
To see the shape, the team used cryoelectron microscopy.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:12.540
Here is the analogy.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.620
Imagine working out the exact shape of a paper lantern

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.800
when all you have is thousands of faint, grainy photos.

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:23.940
Each photo shows an identical lantern frozen at a random angle.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:26.160
No single photo shows much.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.220
Software works out each photo's angle and averages them into one sharp 3D shape,

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:32.140
called a map.

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.440
Then someone builds an atomic model,

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:39.600
threading the protein chain through that map like fitting a wire skeleton inside a plaster cast.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.360
Now, who did what?

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.920
By Anthropic's account, Claude searched about 15 billion predicted proteins for vault relatives.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.940
It turned the chosen sequences into DNA designs ready to order.

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:56.180
The team screened 44 candidates in a test tube expression system.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:01.280
In living E. coli, 15 of the 24 selected proteins came out soluble.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.200
The purification story is the interesting part.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:09.600
Claude first argued against one standard separation step, and the team ran it anyway.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.340
Claude then suggested loading raw cell extract straight onto the column.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:16.840
The researchers hesitated, and it worked.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:22.820
For the microscope data, Claude set up and ran RELION, an open source processing software,

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.380
inside what Anthropic calls an internal multi-agent harness.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:28.400
Researchers steered the analysis.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.520
So the model planned the work and operated software.

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:33.320
It never touched the samples.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:38.100
The bacteria, the microscope, and the people at the bench produce the physical data.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:43.120
Anthropic's own phrase for the arrangement is under our direction.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:49.660
That describes a capable research assistant inside a skilled team, not an autonomous scientist.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:55.980
Here is what makes this kind of claim stronger than most AI for science announcements.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.460
A 3D structure is an artifact, not an opinion.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:04.220
Once the map and model are released, any structural biologist can download them,

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.800
refit the chains, and see whether the model really matches the data.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.000
The field has done this for decades.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:16.340
The rat liver vault, solved by X-ray crystallography in 2009 at 3.5 angstroms,

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:20.060
sits in the public protein data bank with its 78 chains.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.140
That gives a reference for comparing the new particle's architecture.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:26.220
Now, what is missing today?

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.480
Anthropic says it will release the structures and data soon.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.960
We found no database accession numbers.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:37.020
The preprint has been submitted to BioRZiv, but was not yet live when the note came out.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.000
Nothing is peer-reviewed.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.740
The note itself does not state the map's resolution.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.120
One outlet that covered it pointed out the same gap,

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:47.560
along with the missing validation statistics.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:53.740
Anthropic also reports a small molecule in a pocket of each protein copy that it has not identified.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:55.320
That candor helps.

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:56.900
The speed claims are weaker.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.140
The three-day purification is described as what's conventional,

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.100
and the weeks for a model are Anthropic's own estimate.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:08.220
There was no comparison arm, meaning no matched team doing the same work without Claude.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:12.140
The one outside voice, the vault's co-discoverer Leonard Rome,

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:14.540
was brought on by Anthropic as an advisor.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.340
Auto-DNA is a different kind of evidence.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:21.620
It is peer-reviewed in Nature and reports measured numbers.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:24.840
According to a 36KR report on the paper,

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:28.560
AutoDNA matched the enzymatic DNA synthesis yields

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:30.480
optimized by human experts.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:34.220
Across more than 11,000 hardware steps in the project,

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:37.700
it completed an end-to-end DNA data storage cycle

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:40.720
and recovered the original digital content exactly.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.580
It also hit 14 hardware errors.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.480
Six of those needed a human.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:50.080
Humans still chose the experiments and set up the platform.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.200
The same facts support different readings.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:57.660
The AGI forward reading says the important part is breadth.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:03.220
One model contributed to literature search, DNA design, wet lab protocol choices,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:06.600
and image processing, and the whole project took under four months.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.980
On this view, the purification suggestion is the tell.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:14.880
The model argued with experts, proposed something unusual, and turned out to be right.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:19.880
Pair that with a lab-like auto-DNA running the instruments, and the loop starts to close.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:25.540
The A. Realist reading points out that every step Claude touched already had mature tools.

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:28.340
RELION pipelines are well established.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:34.180
A barrel made of 96 identical copies gives the averaging step a lot of symmetry to work with.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:40.120
On this reading, the discovery came from a smart database search followed by ordinary good bench work,

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.740
and the speed-ups are anecdotes with no control.

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:48.560
AutoDNA's own authors note that fully automated work is still mostly standardized tests.

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.580
The market reading looks at incentives

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:56.020
Anthropics Own Life Sciences team ran the search with Claude Science

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:01.640
Which one report calls the company's setup for applying its AI models to scientific research

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:05.800
So these notes are the company describing work done with its own tools

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:07.580
That doesn't make them wrong

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:11.260
It means the checkable artifact matters more than the stopwatch

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:13.300
There is also a policy angle

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:17.140
AutoDNA comes from a Chinese university

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:22.380
and AI that operates biology instruments raises questions about who can intervene

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:25.420
and when during an automated run.

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.220
I read, the structure is probably real and will probably hold up once the data are public.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:36.160
We discount the speed ups until someone measures them against a fair baseline.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:40.180
For builders, the uncomfortable lesson is about where value sits.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.260
Protocol planning and running analysis software

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:46.740
are exactly what frontier labs are now demonstrating in-house.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:54.080
A startup whose product is an agent wrapped around RELION or a protocol planning chat window looks exposed.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:57.140
The harder layers look more defensible.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:04.760
Instrument integration, error recovery, sample handling, and proprietary experimental data are all physical and slow to copy.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:07.340
Autodna's error log makes the point.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:10.100
Six of 14 failures still needed a person.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:12.400
Cost to serve is the open question.

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:17.360
Neither source price is a run, and the expensive parts are likely still microscope time,

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.100
reagents and skilled staff rather than tokens.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.660
One question worth exploring in diligence might be this.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:28.520
What share of experiments run end to end with no human touch,

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:31.040
and what is the cost per successful result?

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:37.060
We'd want that measured against a human-run baseline, the same way, on the same tasks.

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:40.440
A company that can answer with its own logs has something.

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:44.200
A company that cites a vendor's announcement probably does not.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:47.880
Three things would settle this.

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:53.240
First, the preprint going live with the map's resolution and standard validation statistics.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:57.620
Second, the map and model appearing in the public structure databases,

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:00.680
where independent groups can refit and challenge them.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:03.440
Anthropic says that release is coming soon.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.820
Third, an outside lab reproducing the 90-minute purification

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:10.700
or identifying the mystery molecule in the pocket.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.940
That would turn a self-reported speed-up into a method other people use.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:19.240
For auto-DNA, we'd watch for cost per experiment

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:22.640
and for results on messier, non-standard tasks.

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:27.340
We'd change our view if the released data show a weaker map than the note implies

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.360
or if other labs can't reproduce the method.

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:35.240
That's the deep dive and today's AI briefing.

